= Chyavanprash (Cyavanaprāśa)

_A_ classical Ayurvedic compound formula_, not a single substance — the archetypal_ rasāyana leha _(rejuvenative electuary) of the Caraka tradition, named after the sage Cyavana who is said to have been restored from extreme old age by it. This entry treats the formula in the same scholarly register as the single-substance entries; section headings are adapted to fit a multi-ingredient classical preparation rather than a single drug. Status: fully classical in Ayurveda (Caraka Saṃhitā, Cikitsāsthāna 1, the first-named_ rasāyana-yoga _of the canon); not classical in Chinese or Greco-Roman medicine. Ubiquitous in modern Indian Ayurvedic practice as both a classical preparation and a major commercial functional-food._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Sanskrit:* च्यवनप्राश (_cyavanaprāśa_), च्यवनप्राश-अवलेह (_cyavanaprāśa-avaleha_); च्यवन (_cyavana_) — the proper name of the sage; प्राश (_prāśa_) — "eating, food," from √_aś_ "to eat" (with _pra-_ "forth, fully"). The compound thus reads "the food of Cyavana," "what Cyavana ate." Sometimes च्यवनप्राशलेह (_cyavanaprāśa-leha_) or simply च्यवनप्राश-अवलेह, where _leha / avaleha_ means "electuary, lickable medicated paste" — the pharmaceutical class. The sage Cyavana (alt. _Cyavana_) is the son of the ancestor-sage *Bhṛgu* in the Vedic genealogies; the formula bears his name because the *Aśvinīkumāras* (the twin divine physicians of the Vedas) are said to have restored him from decrepit old age and impotence to youthful vigor by means of this preparation — the foundational rejuvenation myth of Ayurveda.#footnote[Etymology and the Cyavana myth: Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _cyavana_, _prāśa_, _avaleha_, √_aś_); the Cyavana / Aśvinīkumāra story is told most fully at Mahābhārata, Vana-parvan 121–125 (the marriage of Cyavana to Sukanyā and his rejuvenation by the Aśvins, who in return are admitted to the soma-sacrifice). The story is also referenced in Caraka Saṃhitā, Cikitsāsthāna 1.62 — the etiology / preamble to the formula's prescription.]

*English:* _Chyavanprash_ (also _Chyawanprash_, _Chyavanaprasha_, _Chyavana-prasha_); transliteration of the Sanskrit. The Hindi-English commercial usage is _chyawanprash_, which has become the dominant modern spelling.

*Chinese:* No classical Chinese name. Modern Chinese: 蓍婆那波拉什 / 印度補膏 (yìndù bǔgāo, "Indian tonifying ointment") — descriptive; no classical drug-form.

== Classical Origin and Authority

Cyavanaprāśa is given the singular honor of being the *first-named rasāyana-yoga* (rejuvenation-formula) in the Caraka Saṃhitā's _rasāyana-adhyāya_ (Cikitsāsthāna 1, _Pāda 1.1: Abhayāmalakīya Rasāyana Pāda_), at verses 62–74. Caraka introduces the formula as "_cyavanaprāśam ity etat paramaṁ rasāyanam_" — "this Cyavanaprāśa is the supreme rejuvenative." It is presented as the foundational formula on which the entire _rasāyana-cikitsā_ tradition is built, suitable for use across all ages and constitutions, and the classical exemplar of the _amalakī-pradhāna_ ("āmalakī-as-chief") class of preparations.#footnote[Caraka Saṃhitā, Cikitsāsthāna 1.1, verses 62–74; the formula is presented in Pāda 1 (_Abhayāmalakīya Rasāyana Pāda_) as the first / supreme _rasāyana-yoga_. Translations: Sharma & Dash, _Caraka Saṃhitā_ (Chowkhamba), vol. III; cf. P. V. Sharma's translation. The classical introduction "_paramaṁ rasāyanam_" is universally cited in commentary as the marker of its foundational status. The classification as _amalakī-pradhāna_ is the dravyaguna pattern-naming (āmalakī as chief ingredient).]

The recipe is also discussed and elaborated in:
- *Suśruta Saṃhitā*, _Cikitsāsthāna_ 28 (rasāyana chapter), with minor variations.
- *Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya*, _Uttarasthāna_ 39, where Vāgbhaṭa gives a slightly modified version.
- *Aṣṭāṅga Saṅgraha* — fuller treatment.
- *Sharangadhara Saṃhitā* — preparation-method details and standardized recipe.
- *Bhaiṣajyaratnāvalī* — the standard modern reference recipe.
- *Ayurvedic Formulary of India*, Part I — the modern Government-of-India standardized recipe (the basis of most commercial preparations).#footnote[Multi-text treatment: Sharma & Dash, _Caraka_ (Chowkhamba); Murthy, _Suśruta_, _Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya_, _Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_, _Bhaiṣajyaratnāvalī_ (Chaukhamba); _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Part I (Government of India publication). Variations across recensions are substantial (40–50+ ingredients depending on the version) — flagged.]

== Composition

The Caraka recipe centers on *āmalakī* (Phyllanthus emblica, the Indian gooseberry — _Emblica officinalis_), with a long supporting cast. Specific ingredient lists vary across recensions and commercial preparations (commonly 40–50+ ingredients), but the *Caraka / Ayurvedic-Formulary canonical version* is built on four families of ingredient:

*The chief / paramount drug (pradhāna):*
- *Āmalakī (आमलकी)* — *Phyllanthus emblica* L., the Indian gooseberry — fresh ripe fruit; ~50% of the formula by mass. The fruit is rich in vitamin C (~600 mg per fresh fruit) and tannin polyphenols; classically the supreme _rasāyana_ fruit (_rasāyana-vara_), tridoṣa-pacifying, with prabhāva of universal applicability.

*The_ daśamūla _("ten roots"; the foundational Vāta-pacifying root combination):*
- *Bilva (Aegle marmelos)* — Bengal quince root.
- *Agnimantha (Premna integrifolia)* — clerodendron.
- *Śyonāka (Oroxylum indicum)* — Indian trumpet flower.
- *Gambhārī (Gmelina arborea)* — beechwood.
- *Pāṭalā (Stereospermum chelonoides)* — Indian bignonia.
The above five = _bṛhat-pañcamūla_ ("five greater roots").
- *Śālaparṇī (Desmodium gangeticum)*.
- *Pṛśniparṇī (Uraria picta)*.
- *Bṛhatī (Solanum indicum)*.
- *Kaṇṭakārī (Solanum xanthocarpum)* — yellow-fruited nightshade.
- *Gokṣura (Tribulus terrestris)* — see _tribulus_ entry.
The above five = _laghu-pañcamūla_ ("five lesser roots").

*The_ jīvanīya / aṣṭavarga _and main rasāyana herbs (the major supporting cast):*
- *Bilva-kṛṣṇabhakta* (or _bilva-majjā_) — wood-apple kernel.
- *Pippalī (Piper longum)* — long pepper; _vājīkara_ and _rasāyana_.
- *Pippali-mūla (Piper longum root)* — long-pepper root.
- *Cavyā (Piper retrofractum)* — Java long pepper.
- *Citraka (Plumbago zeylanica)* — leadwort root.
- *Śuṇṭhī (dried ginger)* — see _ginger_ entry.
- *Punarnavā (Boerhavia diffusa)*.
- *Marica (Piper nigrum)* — black pepper.
- *Vidārī (Pueraria tuberosa)* — Indian kudzu.
- *Aśvagandhā (Withania somnifera)* — see _ashwagandha_ entry.
- *Jīvantī (Leptadenia reticulata)*.
- *Drākṣā (Vitis vinifera)* — raisins.
- *Mudgaparṇī (Phaseolus trilobus)*.
- *Māṣaparṇī (Teramnus labialis)*.
- *Meda, Mahāmeda, Kākolī, Kṣīrakākolī, Ṛddhi, Vṛddhi, Jīvaka, Ṛṣabhaka* — the *aṣṭavarga* "eight orchids," classically a set of high-altitude Himalayan orchids used in rasāyana; *most are now extinct, rare, or replaced by substitutes* in modern preparations.

*The vehicle / base substances:*
- *Ghṛta* (ghee) — clarified butter.
- *Tila taila* (sesame oil) — minor.
- *Madhu* (honey).
- *Śarkarā* (cane sugar) — large quantity.
- *Tugākṣīrī* (Bambusa bamboo silica) — minor.
- *Spices for the finishing aromatic step:* _eḷā_ (cardamom, _Elettaria cardamomum_), _tvak_ (cinnamon, _Cinnamomum verum_), _patra_ (Indian bay leaf, _Cinnamomum tamala_), _nāgakeśara_ (Mesua ferrea stamens).#footnote[The composition list is the standardized Caraka / Ayurvedic Formulary of India recipe; substantial variation exists across recensions and commercial preparations. The _aṣṭavarga_ extinction question is a serious modern Ayurvedic-pharmacy problem: most of the eight Himalayan orchids are CITES-listed and/or genuinely depleted; substitutes (śatāvarī, vidārī, aśvagandhā, kapikacchu, etc.) are commonly used in modern commercial preparations. The classical recipe is therefore not fully reproducible in its original form. Verify against _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Part I, current edition.]

== Preparation method (sāmānya pāka-vidhi)

The classical method, given at Caraka Cikitsāsthāna 1.62–74 and elaborated in _Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_:
1. *First decoction (kvātha):* the daśamūla and supporting roots are coarsely powdered and decocted with water (~16× by volume), reduced to one-quarter (¼).
2. *Āmalakī preparation:* fresh āmalakī fruits are tied in a cloth and boiled in the kvātha until soft; the seeds are removed; the pulp is mashed and strained through cloth.
3. *Frying in ghee (snehana-pāka):* the strained pulp is fried in ghee until aromatic.
4. *Sugar / honey base:* sugar is dissolved in the remaining kvātha and cooked to a thread consistency (_pāka-paryāyā_ — the leha-pāka stage).
5. *Combination:* the fried pulp, sugar syrup, and additional ground fine-spice powder (cardamom, cinnamon, bay leaf, etc.) are mixed; honey is added at a moderate temperature (Ayurveda forbids cooking honey above ~40 °C, which is held to make it _viṣa-tulya_, "poison-equivalent" — this is one of the major _saṃskāra-viruddha_ rules and is observed in classical preparation).
6. *Final consistency:* the resulting paste is a thick, dark brown-to-black sticky electuary (_leha / avaleha_) with the consistency of jam. Stored in earthenware or glass; shelf life many years if properly stored.#footnote[Preparation method: Caraka Cikitsāsthāna 1.62–74; Sharangadhara Saṃhitā Madhyamakhaṇḍa (preparation chapter on _avaleha_); _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Part I. The honey-not-cooked rule (_madhu-pāka-viruddha_) is universal across saṃhitā tradition — see Caraka Sūtrasthāna 27 — and is one of the strictest preparation-rules in Ayurveda; cooked honey is held to be toxic (the modern hypothesis is HMF / hydroxymethylfurfural formation, though the classical doctrine antedates modern chemistry by two millennia).]

== Indications and Uses

Caraka's _rasāyana-adhyāya_ presents Cyavanaprāśa as the *paramaṁ rasāyanam* — supreme rejuvenative — and gives the indications as:
- _Jarā-rogāṇām_ — diseases of old age, in general; the principal classical use.
- _Kāsa, śvāsa_ — cough and dyspnea; one of the principal Caraka-named indications. The respiratory tonic role is a defining property: the formula is the foundational _kāsa-svāsa-hara rasāyana_, and its annual use during cold seasons (autumn-winter, _hemanta-śiśira_) is the traditional Indian winter practice.
- _Kṣaya_ — wasting / consumption (classically including _rājayakṣma_, the Vedic-spectrum wasting disease).
- _Hṛd-roga_ — heart disease (general); cardiac tonic.
- _Smṛti-medhā-vardhana_ — promotes memory and intellect.
- _Vāk-svara-prada_ — improves voice (the voice / speech-tonic action).
- _Varṇya, varcasvī, vayasthāpana_ — improves complexion, lustre, and arrests / delays aging.
- _Vājīkara, śukrala_ — promotes _śukra_ (reproductive vigor); aphrodisiac.
- _Indriya-balya_ — strengthens the senses (sight, hearing).
- _Agni-dīpana_ — kindles digestive fire.
- _Anukūla-balya_ — generally strengthening, anabolic.
- *Daily use as preventive _rasāyana_:* the classical preventive register — taken throughout the cold season (and through the year, by some traditions), particularly by children, elders, and convalescents.
- *Convalescent register:* post-illness recovery, post-partum recovery, exhaustion of any cause.#footnote[Indications: Caraka Saṃhitā, Cikitsāsthāna 1.62–74; reinforced in Suśruta, Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya, and Bhāvaprakāśa. The respiratory-tonic role is the most-emphasized in clinical Ayurvedic practice and is the principal modern Indian commercial framing (jingle: "_calorie_ — _vitamin_ — _Chyawanprash_," i.e. as a nutritional supplement). Translations: Sharma & Dash; P. V. Sharma; Murthy.]

*Doṣa effect (of the compound as a whole):* tridoṣa-pacifying — the canonical claim of Caraka. In practice: the predominant *āmalakī* base gives a Pitta-pacifying register; the *daśamūla / piper-pungent / ghee-honey* base gives a Vāta-pacifying register; the *long-pepper / pippalī / aśvagandhā* warming components prevent Kapha-aggravation in moderate use. Caraka's tridoṣa attribution is the consensus.

*Karma (of the formula):* _rasāyana_, _vājīkara_, _bṛṃhaṇa_, _balya_, _medhya_, _hṛdya_, _kāsa-svāsa-hara_, _agni-dīpana_, _vayasthāpana_, _varṇya_, _indriya-balya_, _smṛti-vardhana_, _vāk-svara-prada_ — the long action-list that defines the foundational _rasāyana-yoga_ status.

== Cautions and Viruddha

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S/N) Conditional cautions:*
  - *Heavy Kapha excess / obesity / metabolic syndrome:* the sugar and ghee content makes Cyavanaprāśa unsuitable in heavy daily doses for these patterns; reduce dose or substitute a sugar-free version.
  - *Diabetes mellitus:* high sugar load — *contraindicated* at standard doses; sugar-free / madhumeha-friendly versions exist but should be used cautiously.
  - *Active _āma_ (undigested food / metabolic toxicity):* the classical rule that _rasāyana_-drugs must wait until _āma_ is cleared (Caraka, Cikitsāsthāna 1) — applies.
  - *Active acute fever / inflammatory illness:* not for the acute phase; reserve for convalescence.
  - *Heavy gut Pitta / acid reflux:* the pippalī / pepper content may aggravate; reduce dose or modify.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Diabetes:* high glycemic load from sugar; modern *sugar-free* variants use jaggery, stevia, or non-nutritive sweeteners and are the option for diabetics — even then, careful glycemic monitoring is required.
  - *Pregnancy:* generally considered safe in moderate doses by classical authority (the rasāyana register for women is endorsed), but the spice / pippalī / aśvagandhā content suggests moderation in the first trimester; consult a clinician.
  - *Sourcing / quality:* commercial preparations vary wildly in adherence to the classical recipe; some are nearly sugar-and-āmalakī-paste alone (cheaper preparations); high-quality preparations from reputable manufacturers (Dabur, Baidyanath, Patanjali, Zandu — among many; quality varies) more closely follow the classical recipe.
  - *Aṣṭavarga substitution:* the original _aṣṭavarga_ ingredients are mostly extinct or CITES-protected; modern commercial preparations substitute, with some loss of classical fidelity (and possible loss of some intended actions). This is a real and significant gap between classical recipe and modern preparation.
  - *Pediatric:* commercial Cyavanaprāśa is marketed for children as a daily winter tonic; the high sugar content makes this an arguable health practice from a modern dental / metabolic perspective; use in moderation.
  - *Drug interactions:* the multi-herb formula has many constituent-level interactions; specifically the *pippalī (Piper longum) and marica (Piper nigrum)* content includes *piperine*, which *substantially inhibits CYP3A4, CYP2C9, and other drug-metabolizing enzymes* and increases the bioavailability of many co-administered drugs — a real and clinically meaningful interaction class. Patients on critical-dose drugs (warfarin, anticonvulsants, immunosuppressants, etc.) should be aware.
  - *Allergens:* contains sesame, dairy (ghee), nuts in some formulations.#footnote[The classical _āma_ and _bṛṃhaṇa_-restriction principles: Caraka Saṃhitā Cikitsāsthāna 1. The aṣṭavarga substitution issue is universally acknowledged in modern Ayurvedic literature — cf. Government of India publications on Ayurvedic herb conservation. Piperine drug-interaction is well documented in modern pharmacology — Shoba et al. (_Planta Medica_, 1998) and subsequent literature. Honey-and-cooking saṃskāra-viruddha: Caraka Sūtrasthāna 27 — classical and observed in proper preparation.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Cyavanaprāśa is adopted into South-Asian Unani in the Mughal-Hakeemi tradition under the_ Maʿjūn _("electuary") class — the foundational Tibb pharmaceutical form that exactly parallels the Ayurvedic_ avaleha / leha. _The formula itself is South-Asian-Unani-adopted as a compound preparation. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ _and_ triphala.typ _(for the parallel Itrīfal adoption pattern)._

*Urdu/Hakeemi:* چیون پراش (_chyavan-prāsh_) — direct phonetic adoption; treated in modern CCRUM publications and the South-Asian Unani polyherbal tradition.

*Tibb classification of the formula class:* the *Maʿjūn (معجون)* class. Cyavanaprāśa fits the Tibb framework as a *Maʿjūn-i Muqawwī al-Jasad wa-l-Rūḥ* (body-and-spirit tonic electuary). The honey + sugar + ghee base is structurally identical to the Unani maʿjūn matrix.

*Mizāj* (of the compound): *Hot and Moist in the 1st degree* (Mughal Unani classification); the predominantly āmalakī-cooling base is moderated by the warming pippalī / aśvagandhā / spice fraction.

*Af'āl:* *Muqawwī al-jasad* (body-strengthening); *muqawwī al-rūḥ al-ḥaywānī* (vital-spirit tonic); *muqawwī al-ṣadr wa-l-ri'a* (chest and lung tonic — the canonical Indian respiratory indication preserved in Unani); *muḥsin al-shaykhūkha* (geriatric-supportive — paralleling jarā-rogāṇām); *muqawwī al-bāh* (aphrodisiac); *mufariḥ*; *muqawwī al-dimāgh* (brain tonic).

*Istemālāt:* geriatric debility; chronic respiratory weakness; convalescence; daily winter tonic; postpartum recovery; sexual debility; memory and intellect support — the indications closely parallel the Ayurvedic register.

*Mawāne':* diabetes; obesity; heavy phlegmatic excess; active hot-pattern fever.

*Muṣliḥ:* the formula is self-corrective by design; if needed, paired with warm milk (*shīr garm*).

*Badal:* *Khamīrah-i Marwārīd Khās* (the Unani pearl-electuary) or other Mufarriḥ-class compounds for cardiac focus; *Itrīfal Aftīmūn* for melancholic.

*Tibb formulas — Unani parallels and equivalents:* the *Maʿjūn-i Falāsifah* ("Philosophers' Electuary") is the closest Greco-Arabic structural parallel — both are honey/sugar-based, multi-ingredient, geriatric-tonic electuaries; the *Maʿjūn-i Salājīt* (with shilajit) and the *Maʿjūn-i Najāḥ* are similar register. The South-Asian Unani tradition adopts Cyavanaprāśa directly as *Maʿjūn Chyavan-prāsh* in modern manufacturer formularies and CCRUM publications.

*Source attestation:* not in Avicenna or Ibn al-Bayṭār (Sanskrit-origin compound formula); fully treated in modern South-Asian Unani literature — *Bayāz-e Kabīr*; CCRUM National Formulary of Unani Medicine; Hakim Akbar Arzani's *Tibb-e Akbarī* discusses adopted Ayurvedic compounds.#footnote[Tibb Maʿjūn Chyavan-prāsh: post-classical Sanskrit-to-Unani adoption, fully integrated in modern South-Asian Unani manufactured formulary.]

== Modern Variations and Commercial Preparations

Cyavanaprāśa is among the most-commercially-produced classical Ayurvedic preparations in the modern Indian market — a multi-billion-rupee category. Major commercial brands:
- *Dabur Chyawanprash* — the largest market share; "Dabur Chyawanprash Awaleha" — the major commercial recipe.
- *Baidyanath Chyawanprash* — long-standing classical preparation.
- *Zandu Chyawanprash*.
- *Patanjali Chyawanprash* — substantial market presence; controversies over ingredient cost-cutting.
- *Sri Sri Ayurveda*, *Vaidyaratnam*, *Kottakkal Arya Vaidya Sala*, *Himalaya*, etc.
- *Government Ayurvedic Pharmacy of various states* — produces a standardized version per the _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_.

*Specialized variants:*
- *Saffron Chyawanprash* — with added saffron; premium variant.
- *Sugar-free Chyawanprash* — replaces sugar with stevia, sorbitol, or jaggery (less classical but more accessible to diabetic users).
- *Children's Chyawanprash* — with reduced spice load, sweeter; targeted at the child market.
- *Brāhmī-enhanced* / *Aśvagandhā-enhanced* / various herbal-emphasis variants.
- *Vegan Chyawanprash* — without ghee; uses sesame or coconut oil; less classical.

*Quality regulation:* the _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_ and the _Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India_ provide reference standards; the Ministry of AYUSH regulates commercial Ayurvedic medicines, and inter-batch quality variation is a real concern.#footnote[Commercial market and quality regulation: Government of India AYUSH ministry publications; Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India; industry literature. The market share leadership of Dabur is well-documented; the price competition has led some manufacturers to cost-cut on aṣṭavarga substitutes and on āmalakī quality, which is a quality concern flagged in the modern Ayurvedic-pharmacy literature.]

== Modern Pharmacology

The compound formula's pharmacology is necessarily complex — the sum of dozens of ingredients. The principal modern-pharmacology-investigated components and their contributions:

*Āmalakī fraction (the principal contributor):*
- *Vitamin C* — substantial in fresh āmalakī; some loss during cooking (the polyphenol matrix protects somewhat); modern studies suggest the ascorbate is more stable than expected.
- *Polyphenols and tannins* — gallic acid, ellagic acid, emblicanins A and B, punigluconin, pedunculagin; substantial antioxidant activity.
- *Cytoprotective and adaptogen* — preclinical evidence.

*Pippalī / marica fraction:*
- *Piperine* — CYP3A4 / CYP2C9 / glucuronidation inhibition; increases bioavailability of many co-administered constituents (and drugs). A significant pharmacological feature.

*Aśvagandhā / vidārī / śatāvarī fraction* — adaptogen-class steroidal saponins (withanolides; vidāri-kanda saponins; shatavarins). See the relevant single-substance entries.

*Daśamūla fraction* — antispasmodic, anti-inflammatory, broad supportive register.

*Honey + ghee + sugar* — caloric base; honey's antimicrobial fraction; ghee's _yogavāhī_ (carrier) classical property + modern bioavailability enhancement of fat-soluble constituents.

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Immunomodulatory / immune-supportive:* small clinical trials (mostly Indian, mixed methodological quality) report improvements in immune markers, reduced respiratory-infection incidence, especially in elderly and pediatric populations.
- *Antioxidant:* substantial in vitro; the polyphenol-rich profile is well-characterized.
- *Anti-inflammatory:* preclinical; clinical evidence preliminary.
- *Respiratory / pulmonary support:* modest evidence for respiratory-infection reduction in winter use; the classical _kāsa-svāsa_ register.
- *Cognitive / memory:* small studies suggest mild improvements; modest effect sizes.
- *Glycemic load:* substantial; the chief modern caveat against routine daily use without consideration.
- *Hepatoprotective:* preclinical animal-model evidence; clinical translation preliminary.
- *Cross-system convergence:* the classical _kāsa-svāsa-hara_ + _rasāyana_ + _vayasthāpana_ profile maps onto the modern respiratory-supportive + antioxidant + immunomodulatory + nutritional cluster; the convergence is reasonable but the modern evidence base is dominated by small Indian trials of variable quality.
- *Adherence vs. effect-attribution problem:* in the daily winter-tonic use, the genuine benefit is plausibly nutritional / vitamin C / antioxidant / classical placebo + suggestion, which is the *combined effect of a daily ritualized warm-milk intake of a polyphenol-rich preparation* — a clinically reasonable practice that is hard to deconstruct into isolated active-ingredient effects.#footnote[Clinical and preclinical evidence: Indian Ayurvedic-research literature (_Indian Journal of Pharmacology_, _Journal of Ayurveda and Integrative Medicine_); review articles in _Phytotherapy Research_ and _Phytomedicine_; verify against current systematic reviews. Methodological quality of Indian studies is variable; substantial industry-funding bias in the commercial-product trials. The cross-system convergence and the "ritualized daily warm-milk antioxidant practice" framing are reasonable modern readings.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Classical adult dose:* 5–15 g/day (one to two teaspoons) with warm milk, in the morning on an empty stomach or as the classical _rasāyana_ ingestion schedule prescribes.
- *Pediatric dose:* ~5 g/day for children >5 years (reduced for younger).
- *Elderly:* 10–15 g/day, with monitoring for digestive tolerance.
- *Risk groups (use cautiously or avoid):* (a) *diabetes mellitus* (use sugar-free version with monitoring, or avoid at standard doses); (b) *heavy obesity / metabolic syndrome*; (c) *active _āma_ / acute inflammatory illness*; (d) concurrent critical-dose drugs (piperine interaction); (e) pregnancy first trimester (use cautiously); (f) honey-allergy / sesame-allergy / nut-allergy (specific formulations).
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges: classical recommendation in Caraka and elaborated in _Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_; current Ayurvedic clinical practice and _Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India_. Reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice.]

